Enzyme are biological catalysts responsible for the speeding up of biochemical reactions. \emph{Catalase} or  \emph{amylase} are two enzymes examples; the former is responsible for breaking down hydrogen peroxide (\ce{H2O2}) whereas the later catalyses the hydrolysis of starch. Enzymes are particularly relevant in biochemistry accelerating chemical reactions at rates necessary for life. Most biochemical reactions requires an enzyme. This section covers the chemical kinetic principles behind biological catalysis and the factors that impact enzime activity.

